1. What is FAISS?

FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) is an open-source library developed by Meta AI Research for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It is written in C++ with complete Python bindings and supports both CPU and GPU execution.

FAISS is designed to handle vector datasets ranging from thousands to billions of entries. It provides multiple index types that allow users to trade off between search accuracy, speed, and memory consumption. FAISS is widely used in production systems for:

Semantic search and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)

Recommendation systems (e.g., ad ranking, content feeds)

Image/video similarity search

Real-time fraud detection (embedding-based anomaly scoring)

Entity resolution and deduplication

The core operation is: given a set of database vectors xᵢ of dimension d, FAISS builds an index structure. When a new query vector x arrives, it efficiently finds argmin‖x − xᵢ‖ (L2 distance) or argmax⟨x, xᵢ⟩ (inner product). FAISS supports exact search (brute-force) and approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search with configurable recall-speed tradeoffs.

2. Core Concepts & Architecture

2.1 Index Lifecycle

Every FAISS index follows a three-phase lifecycle:

Train: For approximate indexes (IVF, PQ), a representative sample of vectors is used to learn cluster centroids or quantization codebooks. Flat and HNSW indexes skip this step.

Add: Database vectors are inserted into the index. For IVF indexes, each vector is assigned to its nearest cluster.

Search: Given query vectors, the index returns the k nearest neighbors along with their distances.

