5. C++ API Integration

5.1 Why C++ for Our Pipeline?

FAISS is natively written in C++. For our low-latency fraud detection and search pipelines — where we already serve XGBoost, ONNX transformer models, and the Rust/C++ gateway from the same process — calling FAISS directly via C++ eliminates Python GIL overhead, HTTP serialization, and enables zero-copy integration with Apache Arrow buffers.

5.2 C++ Usage Pattern

FAISS C++ API mirrors the Python API. Key header: #include <faiss/IndexIVFPQ.h>

// Build IVF-PQ Index
int d = 128;  // embedding dimension
int nlist = 1024;  // number of clusters
int m = 16;  // sub-quantizers
int nbits = 8;  // bits per code

faiss::IndexFlatL2 quantizer(d);
faiss::IndexIVFPQ index(&quantizer, d, nlist, m, nbits);

// Train on representative data
index.train(n_train, train_data.data());

// Add database vectors
index.add(n_db, db_vectors.data());

// Search
int k = 10;
std::vector<float> distances(nq * k);
std::vector<faiss::idx_t> labels(nq * k);
index.nprobe = 32;
index.search(nq, query.data(), k, distances.data(), labels.data());

5.3 C API / FFI for Rust Integration

FAISS provides a pure C interface (c_api/) for FFI integration with Rust, Julia, etc. Build with: cmake -B build -DFAISS_ENABLE_C_API=ON . This produces libfaiss_c.so which can be linked from Rust via bindgen/FFI. Functions use the faiss_ prefix (e.g., faiss_IndexFlat_new). Types use the Faiss prefix (e.g., FaissIndex, FaissMetricType).

5.4 GPU Index in C++

#include <faiss/gpu/GpuIndexIVFPQ.h>
#include <faiss/gpu/StandardGpuResources.h>

faiss::gpu::StandardGpuResources res;
faiss::gpu::GpuIndexIVFPQConfig config;
config.interleavedLayout = true;  // enables cuVS backend

faiss::gpu::GpuIndexIVFPQ gpu_index(
    &res, d, nlist, m, nbits,
    faiss::METRIC_L2, config);

gpu_index.train(n_train, train_data.data());
gpu_index.add(n_db, db_vectors.data());
gpu_index.search(nq, query.data(), k, D.data(), I.data());

