7. Deployment on Kubernetes (On-Prem GPU Cluster)

7.1 Architecture Overview

In our on-prem K8s cluster, FAISS GPU runs as a sidecar or co-located process within the inference pod. The FAISS index is loaded into GPU VRAM at pod startup. The inference service (C++/Rust gateway) calls FAISS directly via in-process C++ API — no network hop.

7.2 Prerequisites

NVIDIA GPU Operator installed (DaemonSet for driver + device plugin)

NVIDIA Device Plugin advertising nvidia.com/gpu resources to scheduler

Container image built with CUDA toolkit + FAISS GPU libraries

GPU nodes labeled: accelerator=nvidia-a100 (or equivalent)

Node Feature Discovery (NFD) for automatic GPU capability labeling

7.3 Pod Spec Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: fraud-detection-scorer
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    accelerator: nvidia-a100
  tolerations:
  - key: nvidia.com/gpu
    operator: Exists
    effect: NoSchedule
  containers:
  - name: scorer
    image: registry.internal/fraud-scorer:latest
    resources:
      limits:
        nvidia.com/gpu: 1
        memory: 32Gi
      requests:
        nvidia.com/gpu: 1
        memory: 16Gi
    volumeMounts:
    - name: faiss-index
      mountPath: /data/faiss
  volumes:
  - name: faiss-index
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: faiss-index-pvc

7.4 Index Loading Strategy

FAISS indexes are serialized to disk using faiss.write_index() (Python) or faiss::write_index() (C++). At pod startup, the index file is read from a PersistentVolume (NFS or local SSD) and loaded into GPU VRAM using index_cpu_to_gpu(). For warm-path workloads, use an init container to pre-download the latest index from the feature store / S3 before the main container starts.

7.5 Host-Level Tuning (for GPU FAISS)

NUMA Pinning: Pin the inference pod to the NUMA node closest to the GPU (via CPU Manager static policy + topology-aware scheduling).

Hugepages: Configure 2MB or 1GB hugepages for large index files to reduce TLB misses during index load.

GPU Isolation: Use MIG (Multi-Instance GPU) on A100 to partition GPU memory if sharing with other workloads.

PCIe Topology: Ensure the GPU and NIC are on the same PCIe root complex to minimize cross-socket traffic.

RMM Memory Pool: Enable RMM pooled allocation in FAISS GPU to avoid repeated cudaMalloc calls.

8. Integration with Our Pipeline

8.1 Fraud Detection — Hot Path (< 5ms)

In the hot path, we use FAISS GPU (IVF-PQ) for real-time similarity search against a transaction embedding index. The flow is:

Rust Gateway receives transaction → extracts features → produces embedding via ONNX (BERT/transformer)

Embedding is passed to FAISS GpuIndexIVFPQ.search() via C++ in-process call

FAISS returns top-K similar historical transactions (with distances) in ~0.3ms

Distances are fed as features into XGBoost ensemble for final fraud score

All data flows via Apache Arrow zero-copy buffers — no serialization overhead

8.2 Search / Ranking Infrastructure (Warm Path)

For Siri-like or LinkedIn-like query processing:

Query embedding is generated by BERT (served via ONNX/C++)

FAISS CAGRA (GPU) retrieves top 50–100 candidate documents/entities in < 1ms

Candidates are re-ranked by a cross-encoder or feature-rich ranker (XGBoost/transformer)

Final ranked list is returned to the user within the 200ms SLA

Index is rebuilt periodically (daily/hourly) using GPU-accelerated CAGRA build, then optionally converted to HNSW for CPU serving if GPU capacity is constrained

8.3 Zero-Copy with Apache Arrow

Both FAISS and our feature pipeline use contiguous float32 arrays. Apache Arrow's columnar format stores embedding columns as contiguous buffers that can be passed directly to faiss::Index::search() without copying. The Rust gateway uses Arrow's C Data Interface to share buffers between Rust, C++, and FAISS. This eliminates serialization/deserialization overhead in the hot path.

9. Operational Best Practices

Index Versioning: Store index files with version tags (e.g., fraud_ivfpq_v42.index). Use blue-green deployment to swap indexes without downtime.

Monitoring: Track search latency (p50/p99), recall@K, GPU VRAM utilization, and QPS via Prometheus + DCGM Exporter.

Index Refresh: Rebuild indexes on a schedule (hourly for fraud embeddings, daily for document search). Use GPU build for speed, then deploy to CPU or GPU serving.

Capacity Planning: 1M × 768d vectors ≈ 3 GB (flat). With PQ (m=64), ≈ 64 MB. Plan GPU VRAM budget accordingly.

Fallback: If GPU is unavailable, FAISS supports transparent CPU fallback via index_gpu_to_cpu(). Ensure CPU indexes are pre-built as backup.

Testing: Validate recall@K against a brute-force Flat index on a held-out query set before deploying a new ANN index.

Multi-GPU Sharding: For datasets > GPU VRAM capacity, shard across multiple GPUs using IndexShards or IndexReplicas (note: cuVS does not support multi-GPU yet — use classic FAISS GPU).

